# Frontend Component Patterns

This document outlines the conventions and patterns for React components in the HeyForm monorepo, covering component structure, TypeScript types, styling, hooks, composition, and best practices.

## Component Structure

### Basic Component Pattern

**Pattern**: Functional components with TypeScript interfaces

```typescript
// ✅ Good: Standard component structure
import { FC, ReactNode } from 'react'
import { cn } from '@/utils'

export interface ButtonProps extends ButtonHTMLAttributes<HTMLButtonElement> {
  size?: 'sm' | 'md' | 'lg'
  loading?: boolean
}

const ButtonComponent: FC<ButtonProps> = ({
  className,
  size = 'lg',
  loading,
  children,
  ...restProps
}) => {
  return (
    <button
      className={cn('base-classes', className)}
      {...restProps}
    >
      {children}
    </button>
  )
}

export const Button = ButtonComponent
```

**Conventions**:
- Use `FC<PropsType>` for functional components
- Export props interface with `export interface ComponentNameProps`
- Use descriptive component names (PascalCase)
- Destructure props with defaults in function signature
- Always spread `...restProps` to pass through HTML attributes
- Export component as named export

### Component File Organization

**Pattern**: One component per file, co-located types

```typescript
// ✅ Good: Component with types in same file
// Button.tsx
export interface ButtonProps { ... }
const ButtonComponent: FC<ButtonProps> = { ... }
export const Button = ButtonComponent

// ✅ Good: Multiple related components in same file
const ButtonComponent: FC<ButtonProps> = { ... }
const GhostButton: FC<ButtonProps> = { ... }
const LinkButton: FC<ButtonProps> = { ... }

export const Button = Object.assign(ButtonComponent, {
  Ghost: GhostButton,
  Link: LinkButton
})
```

**Conventions**:
- One main component per file
- Related variants can be in same file
- Types/interfaces defined above component
- Export component at bottom of file

## TypeScript Types & Interfaces

### Props Interface Patterns

**Pattern**: Extend HTML element attributes with custom props

```typescript
// ✅ Good: Extend HTML attributes
export interface InputProps extends Omit<InputHTMLAttributes<HTMLInputElement>, 'onChange'> {
  hasError?: boolean
  leading?: ReactNode
  trailing?: ReactNode
  onChange?: (value: any) => void
}

// ✅ Good: Extend ComponentProps utility type
interface BadgeProps extends ComponentProps {
  color?: 'red' | 'blue' | 'green'
}

// ✅ Good: Extend DOMProps for components without HTML element
interface ModalProps extends DOMProps {
  open?: boolean
  onOpenChange?: (open: boolean) => void
}
```

**Conventions**:
- Use `ComponentProps<E>` for standard HTML element props (aliased to `HTMLAttributes<E>`)
- Use `DOMProps<E>` for components that only need `children` prop
- Use `Omit<>` to exclude conflicting props (e.g., `onChange`, `children`)
- Export props interfaces with `export interface`
- Use union types for string literals: `'sm' | 'md' | 'lg'`

### Global Type Definitions

**Pattern**: Global types defined in `vite-env.d.ts`

```typescript
// ✅ Good: Global types available everywhere
declare global {
  type DOMProps<E = HTMLElement> = Pick<DOMAttributes<E>, 'children'>
  type ComponentProps<E = HTMLElement> = HTMLAttributes<E>
  type Any = any
  type AnyMap<K = string, V = Any> = Record<K, V>
  type Timeout = ReturnType<typeof setTimeout>
}
```

**Conventions**:
- `ComponentProps<E>` - Standard HTML attributes
- `DOMProps<E>` - Only children prop
- `Any` - TypeScript `any` type
- `AnyMap` - Record type alias
- Use these types consistently across components

### Ref Patterns

**Pattern**: Custom ref interfaces with useImperativeHandle

```typescript
// ✅ Good: Custom ref interface
export interface InputRef {
  clear: () => void
  submit: () => void
}

export interface InputProps extends Omit<InputHTMLAttributes<HTMLInputElement>, 'onChange'> {
  ref?: Ref<InputRef>
  onChange?: (value: any) => void
}

const InputComponent: FC<InputProps> = ({ ref, ...restProps }) => {
  const inputRef = useRef<HTMLInputElement>(null)

  useImperativeHandle<InputRef, InputRef>(
    ref,
    () => ({
      clear() {
        setValue('')
      },
      submit() {
        onEnter?.(inputRef.current?.value)
      }
    }),
    []
  )

  return <input ref={inputRef} {...restProps} />
}
```

**Ref Conventions**:
- Export ref interface: `export interface ComponentNameRef`
- Use `Ref<ComponentRef>` type in props
- Use `useImperativeHandle` to expose custom methods
- Keep internal ref separate from exposed ref
- Include ref in dependency array if needed

## Component Composition

### Object.assign Pattern

**Pattern**: Compose multiple component variants using Object.assign

```typescript
// ✅ Good: Component composition pattern
const ButtonComponent: FC<ButtonProps> = ({ ... }) => { ... }

const GhostButton: FC<ButtonProps> = ({ className, ...restProps }) => (
  <ButtonComponent
    className={cn('border-input bg-foreground', className)}
    {...restProps}
  />
)

const LinkButton: FC<ButtonProps> = ({ className, ...restProps }) => (
  <ButtonComponent
    className={cn('border-0 bg-transparent', className)}
    {...restProps}
  />
)

export const Button = Object.assign(ButtonComponent, {
  Ghost: GhostButton,
  Link: LinkButton
})

// Usage: <Button />, <Button.Ghost />, <Button.Link />
```

**Composition Conventions**:
- Main component as first argument to `Object.assign`
- Variants as properties in second argument object
- Variants wrap main component with different styling/props
- Export composed component as named export
- Use PascalCase for variant names

### Sub-component Pattern

**Pattern**: Nested components for complex structures

```typescript
// ✅ Good: Form with sub-components
const FormComponent: FC<FormProps> = { ... }

const FormItem: FC<FormItemProps> = { ... }

const SimpleForm: FC<SimpleFormProps> = { ... }

export const Form = Object.assign(FormComponent, {
  Item: FormItem,
  Simple: SimpleForm
})

// Usage: <Form />, <Form.Item />, <Form.Simple />
```

**Sub-component Conventions**:
- Use `Object.assign` to attach sub-components
- Sub-components are separate components, not nested
- Export all variants together
- Document usage in component comments

## Styling Patterns

### TailwindCSS with cn Utility

**Pattern**: Use `cn()` utility for conditional classes

```typescript
// ✅ Good: cn utility for class merging
import { cn } from '@/utils'

const ButtonComponent: FC<ButtonProps> = ({
  className,
  size = 'lg',
  loading,
  iconOnly,
  ...restProps
}) => {
  return (
    <button
      className={cn(
        'bg-primary text-primary-light relative h-11 rounded-lg px-3.5',
        {
          '[&_[data-slot=button]]:opacity-0': loading,
          'w-11 px-0': iconOnly
        },
        className
      )}
      {...restProps}
    >
      {children}
    </button>
  )
}
```

**Styling Conventions**:
- Always use `cn()` utility (combines `clsx` + `tailwind-merge`)
- Base classes as first argument (string)
- Conditional classes as second argument (object)
- Custom className as last argument
- Use TailwindCSS utility classes
- Use `data-*` attributes for styling hooks

### Conditional Classes Pattern

**Pattern**: Object syntax for conditional Tailwind classes

```typescript
// ✅ Good: Conditional classes with object
className={cn(
  'base-classes',
  {
    'active-class': isActive,
    'error-class': hasError,
    'disabled-class': disabled
  },
  className
)}

// ✅ Good: Multiple conditions
className={cn(
  'flex items-center gap-x-2',
  {
    'flex-col': vertical,
    'justify-between': spaceBetween,
    'opacity-50': disabled
  },
  className
)}
```

**Conditional Class Conventions**:
- Use object syntax for boolean conditions
- Keep base classes separate from conditionals
- Use descriptive class names
- Group related conditionals together

### Responsive Design

**Pattern**: Mobile-first responsive classes

```typescript
// ✅ Good: Mobile-first responsive classes
className="text-base/6 sm:text-sm/6 h-11 sm:h-10 px-3.5 sm:px-3"

// ✅ Good: Responsive with data attributes
className="h-11 data-[size=md]:h-9 data-[size=sm]:h-9 sm:h-10 data-[size=md]:sm:h-9"
```

**Responsive Conventions**:
- Mobile-first approach (base = mobile, `sm:` = desktop)
- Use `sm:` breakpoint for tablet/desktop
- Group responsive variants together
- Use data attributes for component-level variants

## React Hooks Patterns

### useState Pattern

**Pattern**: Local state with TypeScript types

```typescript
// ✅ Good: Typed useState
const [value, setValue] = useState<string>('')
const [loading, setLoading] = useState(false)
const [state, setState] = useState<'idle' | 'pending' | 'success' | 'error'>('idle')

// ✅ Good: State from props with sync
const [value, setValue] = useState(rawValue)

useEffect(() => {
  if (rawValue !== value) {
    setValue(rawValue)
  }
}, [rawValue])
```

**useState Conventions**:
- Provide TypeScript type for complex types
- Use union types for state machines
- Sync with props using `useEffect`
- Use descriptive state variable names

### useCallback Pattern

**Pattern**: Memoize event handlers and callbacks

```typescript
// ✅ Good: useCallback for event handlers
const handleChange = useCallback(
  (newValue: string) => {
    onChange?.(newValue)
    onValuesChange?.(changes, values)
  },
  [onChange, onValuesChange, changes, values]
)

// ✅ Good: useCallback with dependencies
const handleConfirm = useCallback(async () => {
  onConfirm?.()
  if (fetch) {
    setTrue()
    try {
      await fetch()
      onFinish?.()
    } catch (err: Any) {
      setError(err)
    }
    setFalse()
  }
}, [fetch, onConfirm, onFinish, setError, setFalse, setTrue])
```

**useCallback Conventions**:
- Use for event handlers passed to children
- Use for callbacks in dependency arrays
- Include all dependencies in array
- Use `?.()` for optional callbacks
- Name handlers with `handle` prefix

### useMemo Pattern

**Pattern**: Memoize expensive computations

```typescript
// ✅ Good: useMemo for computed values
const columns = useMemo(
  () =>
    rawColumns.map(c => ({
      _key: (c.field as AnyMap)[rowKey],
      ...c
    })),
  [rawColumns, rowKey]
)

// ✅ Good: useMemo for filtered lists
const list = useMemo(
  () =>
    (data?.list || []).map(r => ({
      _key: r[rowKey],
      ...r
    })),
  [data?.list, rowKey]
)
```

**useMemo Conventions**:
- Use for expensive array/object transformations
- Use for filtered/mapped data
- Include all dependencies
- Return transformed data structure

### useEffect Pattern

**Pattern**: Side effects with cleanup

```typescript
// ✅ Good: useEffect with cleanup
useEffect(() => {
  return () => {
    cancel()
  }
}, [])

// ✅ Good: Sync with props
useEffect(() => {
  if (rawValue !== value) {
    setValue(rawValue)
  }
}, [rawValue])

// ✅ Good: Side effect with dependencies
useEffect(() => {
  onLoadingChange?.(loading)
}, [loading, onLoadingChange])
```

**useEffect Conventions**:
- Return cleanup function for subscriptions
- Include all dependencies in array
- Use optional chaining for callbacks
- Sync state with props when needed

### useRef Pattern

**Pattern**: Refs for DOM access and mutable values

```typescript
// ✅ Good: useRef for DOM elements
const inputRef = useRef<HTMLInputElement>(null)

useEffect(() => {
  if (inputRef.current && autoFocus) {
    inputRef.current.focus()
  }
}, [inputRef])

// ✅ Good: useRef for mutable values
const lock = useRef(false)
const timerRef = useRef<Timeout>(undefined)

function handleCompositionStart() {
  lock.current = true
}
```

**useRef Conventions**:
- Type refs with element type: `useRef<HTMLInputElement>(null)`
- Use for DOM element access
- Use for mutable values that don't trigger re-renders
- Access via `.current` property

## Event Handlers

### onChange Pattern

**Pattern**: Custom onChange with value extraction

```typescript
// ✅ Good: Custom onChange handler
export interface InputProps extends Omit<InputHTMLAttributes<HTMLInputElement>, 'onChange'> {
  onChange?: (value: any) => void
}

const InputComponent: FC<InputProps> = ({ onChange, ...restProps }) => {
  function handleChange(event: ChangeEvent<HTMLInputElement>) {
    let newValue = event.target.value

    // Type-specific processing
    if (type === 'number') {
      newValue = Number(newValue)
      if (helper.isNan(newValue)) {
        newValue = rawValue
      }
    }

    onChange?.(newValue)
  }

  return <input onChange={handleChange} {...restProps} />
}
```

**onChange Conventions**:
- Extract value from event
- Process value based on type
- Call optional callback with processed value
- Use `?.()` for optional callbacks

### Composition Events

**Pattern**: Handle IME composition events

```typescript
// ✅ Good: Composition event handling
const lock = useRef(false)

function handleCompositionStart() {
  lock.current = true
}

function handleCompositionEnd(event: CompositionEvent<HTMLInputElement>) {
  lock.current = false
  onChange?.(getValue(event))
}

function handleChange(event: ChangeEvent<HTMLInputElement>) {
  if (!lock.current) {
    onChange?.(getValue(event))
  }
}

return (
  <input
    onCompositionStart={handleCompositionStart}
    onCompositionEnd={handleCompositionEnd}
    onChange={handleChange}
  />
)
```

**Composition Conventions**:
- Use ref to track composition state
- Lock onChange during composition
- Call onChange on composition end
- Prevents duplicate onChange calls for IME

### Keyboard Events

**Pattern**: Handle keyboard interactions

```typescript
// ✅ Good: Keyboard event handler
function handleKeyDown(event: KeyboardEvent<HTMLInputElement>) {
  if (!lock.current && event.key === 'Enter') {
    onEnter?.(getValue(event as Any))
  }
}

return <input onKeyDown={handleKeyDown} {...restProps} />
```

**Keyboard Conventions**:
- Check composition lock before handling
- Use `event.key` for key detection
- Call optional callbacks with `?.()`
- Extract value before calling callback

## Data Fetching & Async Patterns

### Async Component Pattern

**Pattern**: Async data fetching with loading/error states

```typescript
// ✅ Good: Async component with states
export interface AsyncProps {
  loader?: ReactNode
  cacheFirst?: boolean
  fetch: () => Promise<boolean>
  refreshDeps?: any[]
  emptyRender?: (props: EmptyRenderProps) => ReactNode
  errorRender?: (err: Error) => ReactNode
  children: ReactNode
}

export const Async: FC<AsyncProps> = ({
  loader,
  cacheFirst = false,
  fetch,
  refreshDeps,
  emptyRender,
  errorRender,
  children
}) => {
  const [state, setState] = useState<'idle' | 'pending' | 'success' | 'error'>('idle')

  const { data, error, runAsync: refresh, cancel } = useRequest(
    async () => await fetch(),
    {
      refreshDeps,
      onBefore() {
        setState('pending')
      },
      onSuccess() {
        setState('success')
      },
      onError() {
        setState('error')
      }
    }
  )

  useEffect(() => {
    return () => {
      cancel()
    }
  }, [])

  switch (state) {
    case 'idle':
      return cacheFirst ? children : null
    case 'pending':
      return cacheFirst ? children : loader
    case 'success':
      return !data && emptyRender ? emptyRender({ refresh }) : children
    case 'error':
      return errorRender ? errorRender(error as Error) : children
    default:
      return null
  }
}
```

**Async Conventions**:
- Use state machine for async states
- Use `useRequest` hook from `ahooks`
- Provide loader, empty, and error render props
- Cancel requests on unmount
- Support cache-first mode

### Form Fetching Pattern

**Pattern**: Form submission with loading/error states

```typescript
// ✅ Good: Form with fetch integration
const SimpleForm: FC<SimpleFormProps> = ({
  fetch,
  showFetchError = true,
  submitProps: { label, ...restSubmitProps },
  ...restProps
}) => {
  const { loading, error, runAsync } = useRequest(fetch!, {
    manual: true,
    refreshDeps: [fetch, ...refreshDeps]
  })

  async function handleFinish(values: any) {
    if (onFinish) {
      onFinish(values)
    } else {
      await runAsync(values)
    }
  }

  return (
    <Form onFinish={handleFinish} {...restProps}>
      {children}

      <div data-slot="submit">
        <Button
          type="submit"
          loading={loading}
          data-slot="submit-button"
          {...restSubmitProps}
        >
          {label}
        </Button>
      </div>

      {showFetchError && error && !loading && (
        <div className="text-error !mt-1 text-sm/6" data-slot="form-error">
          {error.message}
        </div>
      )}
    </Form>
  )
}
```

**Form Fetching Conventions**:
- Use `useRequest` with `manual: true`
- Show loading state on submit button
- Display error message below form
- Use `data-slot` attributes for styling hooks
- Support both `fetch` prop and `onFinish` callback

## Loading States

### Loading Prop Pattern

**Pattern**: Loading state with visual feedback

```typescript
// ✅ Good: Loading prop with loader
const ButtonComponent: FC<ButtonProps> = ({
  loading,
  disabled,
  children,
  ...restProps
}) => {
  return (
    <button
      className={cn({
        '[&_[data-slot=button]]:opacity-0': loading
      })}
      disabled={loading || disabled}
      {...restProps}
    >
      <div data-slot="button">{children}</div>

      {loading && (
        <div className="absolute inset-0 flex items-center justify-center" data-slot="loader">
          <Loader />
        </div>
      )}
    </button>
  )
}
```

**Loading Conventions**:
- Accept `loading` boolean prop
- Disable component when loading
- Hide content with opacity when loading
- Show loader overlay when loading
- Use `data-slot` for styling hooks

## Error Handling

### Error Display Pattern

**Pattern**: Error messages with styling

```typescript
// ✅ Good: Error display
{showFetchError && error && !loading && (
  <div className="text-error !mt-1 text-sm/6" data-slot="form-error">
    {error.message}
  </div>
)}

// ✅ Good: Error state in form item
const hasError = meta.errors.length > 0

<div
  className={className}
  data-slot="item"
  data-error={hasError ? '' : undefined}
>
  {hasError && (
    <div className="text-error text-sm/6" data-slot="error">
      {meta.errors[0]}
    </div>
  )}
</div>
```

**Error Conventions**:
- Use `text-error` class for error text
- Show errors only when not loading
- Use `data-error` attribute for styling hooks
- Display first error message
- Use `data-slot` for error container

